@namespace SpaceSails.Client.Components
@using SpaceSails.Client.Rendering

@* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   #997 · THE CAPPED SCROLL PANEL — a head that keeps its measured height and a body that takes what is
   left and scrolls there.

   EXTRACTED FROM #993, not invented here. The owner, docked at The Red Eye with a four-step plan and the
   last row's editor open: "Let's make the step list start scroll when it gets high enough." The Plotting
   panel had a `max-height: calc(100vh - 14rem)` standing in for the chrome above it, and on the Nav desk
   that chrome is 34rem — the cap never engaged once, from the day it was written. #993's answer was to
   stop guessing and let flex measure: the panel gives (`flex: 0 1 auto; min-height: 0`), its head does not
   (`flex: 0 0 auto`), and exactly one child — the one with a scroll in it — takes the remainder
   (`flex: 1 1 auto; min-height: <floor>; overflow-y: auto; overscroll-behavior: contain`).

   That arithmetic was written once, for one panel, in a comment forty lines long. This is the same
   arithmetic as a component, so the next panel that needs it does not get a fresh guess at a constant.

   THE PANEL'S OWN `overflow-y: auto` STAYS as the backstop: on a very short window the body reaches its
   floor, stops giving, and the whole panel pages rather than squeezing the list to nothing.
   ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ *@

<div class="capped-scroll @HostClass" @attributes="Splat" style="@FloorStyle">
    @Header
    @if (HasContent)
    {
        <div class="capped-scroll-body @BodyClass">@ChildContent</div>
    }
</div>
